      {
        "id": "p46-q69-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $s_0=2$ and $s_1=-p$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$s_0=2$ and $s_1=-p$, using the definition and the sum of roots.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}s_0&=\\alpha^0+\\beta^0\\\\s_0&=1+1=2.\\end{align*}$$\n\nBy the sum of roots of $x^2+px+q=0$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}s_1&=\\alpha+\\beta\\\\s_1&=-p.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p46-q69-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $s_k$ satisfies\n\n$$s_k=-ps_{k-1}-qs_{k-2},$$\n\nfor $k\\geq2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$s_k=-ps_{k-1}-qs_{k-2}$ for $k\\geq2$.\n\nMultiply the two root equations by $\\alpha^{k-2}$ and $\\beta^{k-2}$ respectively, then add.",
          "worked": "Since $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are roots,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha^2&=-p\\alpha-q\\\\\\beta^2&=-p\\beta-q.\\end{align*}$$\n\nMultiply by $\\alpha^{k-2}$ and $\\beta^{k-2}$ respectively:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha^k&=-p\\alpha^{k-1}-q\\alpha^{k-2}\\\\\\beta^k&=-p\\beta^{k-1}-q\\beta^{k-2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nAdd the equations:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha^k+\\beta^k&=-p(\\alpha^{k-1}+\\beta^{k-1})-q(\\alpha^{k-2}+\\beta^{k-2})\\\\s_k&=-ps_{k-1}-qs_{k-2},\\qquad k\\geq2.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p46-q69-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $s_3=3pq-p^3$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$s_3=3pq-p^3$.\n\nUse the recurrence to find $s_2=p^2-2q$, then substitute into the recurrence for $s_3$.",
          "worked": "Using $s_0=2$ and $s_1=-p$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}s_2&=-ps_1-qs_0\\\\s_2&=-p(-p)-q(2)\\\\s_2&=p^2-2q.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\\begin{align*}s_3&=-ps_2-qs_1\\\\s_3&=-p(p^2-2q)-q(-p)\\\\s_3&=3pq-p^3.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
